          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "babcock_forceps_subsection_13",
          "subsection_title": "Final OSCE presentation",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "babcock_forceps_group_14",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "If you are handed Babcock forceps at a surgical instrument station, you can present it as follows:"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "“This is a Babcock tissue forceps. It is a grasping and holding instrument designed for relatively atraumatic handling of delicate tissues. It typically has ring handles, a ratchet-locking mechanism, a box joint and broad, rounded, fenestrated jaws. It is used in procedures such as appendicectomy, laparotomy and selected bowel and gynaecological surgeries. Compared with Allis forceps, it generally causes less tissue trauma when correctly used. Complications of improper application include tissue crushing, tearing and ischaemic injury.”"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "group_id": "babcock_forceps_group_15",
              "group_title": "Last-minute revision checklist",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Identify the instrument by its fenestrated jaws."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Classify it as a grasping and holding instrument."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "State its primary function: relatively atraumatic tissue handling."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Know the appendix and bowel as classic examples."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Distinguish it from Allis forceps."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Mention tissue injury from excessive pressure as a complication."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Explain why gentle traction and appropriate jaw pressure matter."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
